{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q026_protein_kinase_like_function_transfer","answer":"In SCOPe 2.08, all three catalytic domains are in the alpha+beta class d, protein kinase-like fold d.144, and protein kinase-like superfamily d.144.1. This superfamily placement supports remote common ancestry—not merely similar shape—and a conserved kinase-like catalytic architecture compatible with nucleotide-dependent phosphoryl transfer. They are nevertheless separate families: phosphoinositide 3-kinase catalytic domains d.144.1.4 (SUNID 56171), aminoglycoside/APH phosphotransferases d.144.1.6 (SUNID 64411), and canonical protein-kinase catalytic subunits d.144.1.7 (SUNID 88854).

The shared superfamily justifies hypotheses about the catalytic core, ATP handling, phosphoryl-transfer chemistry, and use of a family member as a remote structural template for the conserved core. It does not justify transferring substrate specificity, physiological function, inhibitor sensitivity, catalytic efficiency, or regulation: canonical kinases generally modify protein residues, APHs modify particular positions on aminoglycosides, and PI3Ks phosphorylate membrane phosphoinositides. Nor should activation-loop control from protein kinases, antibiotic-resistance specificity from APHs, or membrane recruitment and regulatory-subunit control from PI3Ks be projected across families. Family-specific loops, pockets, insertions, regulatory domains, interfaces, and conformational states are unsafe template targets.

Such transfer requires additional, target-specific evidence: significant sequence/profile homology with conservation of catalytic and specificity-determining residues; domain-boundary-aware structural alignment showing compatible active-site geometry; preferably ligand-, nucleotide-, or substrate-bound structures; biochemical substrate-panel and kinetic assays plus catalytic-residue mutagenesis; and, for regulation, evidence from full domain architecture, phosphorylation or other modifications, interaction partners, membrane association, localization, and cellular genetics. Templates or representatives should be chosen from the closest relevant family and matched for ligand/conformational state and domain context; a canonical kinase representative is not automatically suitable for APH or PI3K specificity modeling.","evidence_used":["pretrained model knowledge","mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info: /api/v1/, /api/v1/releases/current","mcp__scope__search_scop: /api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=aminoglycoside+phosphotransferase&mode=auto&limit=50, /api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=protein+kinase&mode=token&limit=50, /api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=PI3K&mode=token&limit=50","mcp__scope__get_scop_node: /api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/d.144, /api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/d.144/parents, /api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/d.144.1, /api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/d.144.1/parents","mcp__scope__list_scop_children: /api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/d.144.1/children?limit=50"]}]}
